   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the mental hygiene law, in relation to designating the ombudsman as an employee of the commission on quality of care and advocacy for persons with disabilities",
    "basePrintNo": "S5792",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S5792-2011",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §13.34, Ment Hyg L",
    "lawSection": "Mental Hygiene Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S5792\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\nAn act\nto amend the mental hygiene law, in relation to\ndesignating the ombudsman\nas an employee of the commission on quality of care and advocacy for\npersons with disabilities\n\nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\nThe purpose of this bill is to\nreinforce that the Office for People with Developmental Disabilities\nombudsman is an independent advocate by making them an employee of\nthe Commission on Quality of Care and Advocacy for Persons with\nDisabilities.\n\nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\nSection 1 of the bill makes the Office\nfor People with Developmental Disabilities ombudsman an employee of\nthe Commission on Quality of Care and Advocacy for Persons with\nDisabilities.\n\nJUSTIFICATION:\nThe New York Times recently published an article which\nchronicled allegations of abuse and mistreatment of residents with\ndevelopmental disabilities in group homes. As a result, the Assembly\nheld a series of statewide public hearings aimed at carefully\nexamining the quality of care and safety measures in homes for\nindividuals' with developmental disabilities.\n\nUnder current law, each district office of the Office for People with\nDevelopmental Disabilities has an ombudsman that is employed by the\noffice. The ombudsman's duties include advocating for people\nreceiving services at state facilities. The Commission on Quality of\nCare and Advocacy for Persons with Disabilities has a similar role\nwith all the mental hygiene agencies. Making the ombudsman an\nemployee of the Commission rather than the Office for People with\nDevelopmental Disabilities will allow the ombudsman and Commission to\nbetter pool resources and coordinate efforts in advocating for\npersons with developmental disabilities.\n\nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\nNew bill.\n\nFISCAL IMPACT:\nNone.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect on the ninetieth day after\nit shall have become a law.\n\n",
